WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
If we show you a random shape, your eyes&nbsp;
will send the information to your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.220
which will then decide what to&nbsp;
do with it - perhaps not much.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.460
If we show you another shape, your&nbsp;
senses will again send it to your brain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.680
but now it might be able to find some related&nbsp;
information that was stored in your memory.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.240
What’s going on here?

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.720
You may have forgotten, but when you&nbsp;
were young, you knew nothing. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.240
first time you saw a particular shape, it was&nbsp;
meaningless. Over time and in search for order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:39.240
you started to recognize patterns. One day&nbsp;
someone gave that particular thing meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.220
your mind labeled it as important and&nbsp;
decided to store it for future use.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.400
The next time you see that thing,&nbsp;
you recognize it and link it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.200
a previous experience. The more often&nbsp;
you make such meaningful experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.420
the more data will be stored in your memory&nbsp;
and your understanding of that matter grows.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.380
And one day, when you see that particular&nbsp;
shape you’ll say “it’s an ape!”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.160
and if you give your mind some&nbsp;
time to look up all those memories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:16.560
it might come up with lots of things you&nbsp;
can relate to this one piece of digital ink.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.960
So how does your memory work?

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.980
The multi-store model asserts that&nbsp;
the human brain has three processors.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.580
A sensory register, where new information enters.&nbsp;

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.080
a short term memory, which&nbsp;
quickly assesses the input, and&nbsp;

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.100
a long-term store, where it can&nbsp;
potentially be held forever.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.920
We need attention to get things to the&nbsp;
short-term memory, rehearsal to hold it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.680
there for longer and transfer for sending it&nbsp;
to long term storage — and bringing it back.&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.700
Let’s now try to understand&nbsp;
how everything interacts.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.960
Our life is a continuous stream of stimuli&nbsp;
that we perceive in different ways.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.200
There are our senses such as vision, touch,&nbsp;
smell, hearing and taste. There are internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.280
states such as stress, sleepiness, or hunger.&nbsp;
And there are emotions, wants and intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.220
All of them are part of the sensory input&nbsp;
that our brain has to process all the time.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.320
The sensory register can perceive any such new&nbsp;
stimuli. They are then filtered by attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:41.100
processes and decomposed into manageable chunks.&nbsp;
These chunks of information are then sent further.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.620
The short-term memory holds the new info for a&nbsp;
moment. Then it will either forget it, transfer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.180
it to the long term memory, or instantly&nbsp;
use it by bringing it to our attention.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.580
Also known as working memory, our&nbsp;
attention can hold about 7 bits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.640
information for a few seconds to complete a task.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.540
If we pay attention, our brain can&nbsp;
send fresh memories to the long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.940
store. This happens especially well&nbsp;
when we rest. During sleep we tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:19.320
to consolidate important things and&nbsp;
discard others — which we then forget.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:28.260
The Long-term memory is a vast library of&nbsp;
stories, experiences, facts and concepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.420
some of which we can recall for life, and&nbsp;
others we are unable to remember the next day.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.280
Inside this long-term store are&nbsp;
again different types of memories.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:46.260
There are episodic memories which are chunks of&nbsp;
detailed information with a high emotional value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.680
Such as all your stories, some of which you might&nbsp;
be able to recall in great detail. Like the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.800
day of school or your first kiss. Maybe you can&nbsp;
even recall some specific thoughts from that day.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.100
There are semantic memories, things&nbsp;
like facts or general knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:10.260
Repeated episodic memories, like the&nbsp;
way to school, can also, over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.140
turn into semantic information. You can think&nbsp;
of such memories as a large network of ideas:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:22.740
If you see a particular shape, you may&nbsp;
think: gorilla - ape - human - intelligence.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.000
Episodic and semantic memories are&nbsp;
known as explicit memories. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.460
conscious recollections. They are all the&nbsp;
information and experiences we know we hold.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:43.200
There are also implicit memories, skills&nbsp;
and things you are not aware of. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.540
process of walking relies on muscle memory.&nbsp;
You’ve learned it by trying and failing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.900
and today you have no idea how you actually do&nbsp;
it. Implicit memories are also at work when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.460
unconsciously react to a stimulus&nbsp;
— a process known as conditioning.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.500
To make things more complicated, our memories&nbsp;
often change. Sometimes we forget parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:12.600
sometimes we add new details to old&nbsp;
memories in response to how we feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:17.340
right now. When we think of a holiday we&nbsp;
took with someone we don't like anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:21.540
a trip that was actually nice, might&nbsp;
be remembered as unpleasant today.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:29.100
So next time you see this or that shape,&nbsp;
you might want to pause and pay tribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.420
to the fact that there are more connections&nbsp;
in your brain than stars in the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:38.700
here to help you organize inputs, thoughts&nbsp;
and memories to get you through life.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:46.320
Now, the best thing about memories is&nbsp;
still creating them! Trying new things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.060
failing and then trying them again&nbsp;
is one good way to do just that!

